(Beijing): "Smoking is bad for health" is a slogan that people around the world are well aware of. However, there are still nearly 8 million people worldwide who die from tobacco use every year.
Research by a number of major global institutions working on public health issues has shown that the use of tobacco products poses a high risk to the health of consumers and second-hand smokers. Not only that, every year, tobacco has greatly affected the family life and the national economy.
In 2016, China launched the "Healthy China 2030" plan to promote the construction of a healthy China and improve the health of the people, with efforts to gradually improve the mechanism. Concerned about the health and well-being of the people, the focus is on promoting the integrity of the health system by boosting the coverage of the social security system, especially health insurance.
In fact, the habit of throwing cigarette butts outdoors has become a major factor polluting the environment, as cigarette butts contain toxic plastic that, when exposed to sunlight and moisture, emits toxic particles into the air. Soil and food, these are easily transmitted agents to humans and animals without our knowledge.
Cambodia has also worked for many years to control smoking. The Ministry of Health of Cambodia has issued several proclamations on the banning of smoking or fumigation of tobacco products to be implemented in workplaces, shops, restaurants, public places, etc., so as not to affect the public health. Cigarette packs must also be labeled as standard.
