North Korea stockpiles Chinese mask and vaccine ahead of first outbreak of Kovid-19
Two weeks ago, North Korea reported its first outbreak of COVID-19, raising serious concerns about a shortage of vaccines, medical resources and the food crisis in the country. More than 25 million people.
From January to April, data showed that North Korea bought more than 10.6 million panels from China, and in the past four months, China also exported nearly 95,000 thermometers. Compared to 2021, the amount is more than 33 times.
China also shipped 1,000 respirators to North Korea in April worth $ 266,891, as well as medical equipment that could be used to test for COVID-19, including condoms. Rubber gloves and protective clothing. China's total exports to North Korea reached $ 98.1 million in April, the highest since January 2020.
North Korea has closed its borders to almost all trade to prevent the spread of COVID-19 and has just allowed the supply and import of some products from China by train and ship. Only recently.
Training of trainers on the process of establishing an inter-generational and multifunctional elderly association in Battambang province has been successfully completed
(Battambang): The Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, in collaboration with a number of NGOs, has completed the training of trainers on the "process of establishing inter-generational and multifunctional elderly associations" for officials from eight capital-provincial social affairs departments. Successfully.
On that occasion, Mr. Vorn Bun Reth made a report on the results of the five-day training, which received good results with the cooperation and guidance of the Ministry of Social Affairs to facilitate the successful completion.
Mr. Pheng Sethy thanked the Ministry of Social Affairs for its cooperation with the Elderly Organization, a partner organization of the Royal Government, which has always helped to alleviate social work in all fields and has been able to implement this work in the long run. "If the old people teach the young people, it will be easy, but if the children teach the old people, it will be a little difficult, so we have to be a little patient so that the work can be done successfully," he said.
This course also shows the purpose of the growth of the elderly population in Cambodia is an important issue that needs the attention of all stakeholders to contribute to the prevention and response to any challenges in this work. Coming soon.
If Cambodia does not prepare and prepare a proper and timely response, Cambodia will face a crisis in a society with a large number of elderly people and will be responsible for solving many problems such as health, economic, social security and other issues. . Although the Royal Government of Cambodia has shown concern and has set out a national policy on the elderly 2017-2030, has been implementing the second phase of the strategic plan.
In line with the priority policy of the National Policy on the Elderly, the Association of the Elderly (OPA) is a grassroots organization working for the well-being of the elderly and their communities. Recognizing the importance of senior citizens 'associations for rural development, the Royal Government of Cambodia adopted this model in 2013 and issued national guidelines for the establishment and management of senior citizens' associations.
