Myanmar's central bank orders state institutions not to use foreign currency (Video inside): Myanmar's central bank has ordered ministries and local governments not to use foreign currency for domestic trading operations to ease pressure on the currency. . According to Reuters.
The Southeast Asian nation's economy has been in crisis since the military seized power last year, hampering political and economic reforms that have taken place over the past decade and put pressure on the country's exchange rate. Against the US dollar.
"In addition to the growing demand for foreign currency, the exchange rate may fluctuate sharply due to performance," Win Thaw, deputy governor of the Central Bank of Myanmar, said in a statement issued on Wednesday. Accept and spend foreign currency for the purchase of goods and services in the country.
Mr Win Thaw said institutions such as hotels, restaurants, souvenir shops and international schools now use dollars instead of coins, but also businesses and institutions run by state ministries use dollars.
In the same statement sent to the ministry and local government, he said: "Myanmar's currency should be used for domestic payments and each ministry, local government and central government ... should Introduce your unit as needed. ”
The order is the latest in a series of authorities' efforts to control more foreign currency flowing into the military-ruled country.
The official exchange rate of the Central Bank of Myanmar for the current rate is 1,850 per dollar, but the exchange rate is much lower than the informal black market rate. According to Reuters.
China was the last major country to adopt a large-scale Kovid-19 test policy and rapid closure to eradicate community-based infections, but the crackdown has destroyed businesses and companies.
Restrictions on dozens of cities in recent months, including Shenzhen, a manufacturing hub, and Jilin, have disrupted supply chains and put economic indicators on hold. Lowest in about 2 years.
"We are currently at a critical juncture in determining economic trends throughout the year," he said. "We have to seize the opportunity and try to get the economy back on track."
Li's remarks are the latest call from officials and business leaders to strike a balance between taking steps to stop the infection and helping the struggling economy.
China's retail sales in April fell 11.1 percent from April last year, while factory output fell 2.9 percent, the worst showing since the first days of the Kovid crisis.
The city's unemployment rate returned to a record low in February 2020, a challenge for policymakers to achieve their full-year growth target of 5.5 percent. According to the AFP news agency.
