Tomorrow morning, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol came out to answer questions related to electric vehicles live on the Ministry's Facebook page.
The show will air live from 9 a.m. to 11 p.m. Citizens can ask questions directly to the program by dropping questions into the comments in this live program.
Please stay tuned to watch this live broadcast to get the latest news related to the use of electric vehicles as well as the progress of the work of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport.
The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, has been promoting the use of electric vehicles to contribute to the environment by reducing air pollution from fuel or gas vehicles.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that the use of electric vehicles benefits both the environment and the family economy, saving up to three times the cost of using fuel-efficient cars.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol has stressed that Cambodia is committed to reducing air pollution by 27 percent by 2030 and to zero by 2050. Therefore, the use of electric vehicles is part of reducing pollution.
The Minister of Public Works and Transport claimed that in order to promote the use of electric vehicles, the Royal Government and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport have launched a number of programs. In that, the Royal Government has decided to impose a lower import tax on electric cars than cars running on fuel. Fuel-powered cars are required to pay about 120 percent tax on the price of cars, while electric cars pay only 63 percent import tax.
At the same time, the Ministry has already discussed with various gas stations to establish power stations at gas stations, such as at the Kal Tech gas station on Prey Sar Road.
Minister Sun Chanthol also said that in the future he plans to talk with food transport companies to choose electric motorcycles for transport. Mr. Sun Chanthol is optimistic that in the future, the use of electric vehicles will increase in Cambodia.
According to Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, the use of electric vehicles is currently on the rise in Cambodia. In the first three months of 2022, there was an increase of 700 percent compared to the first three months of 2021, from 7 units to 47 units. Authorities send woman who abused her adopted daughter to court
