World Bank Provides $ 169 Million to Support Road Safety and Climate Resilience in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The World Bank has approved a $ 169 million grant for a new financing package for the Southeast Asia Disaster Risk Management Project in Cambodia Phase II to increase the resilience of rural road networks to natural disasters; Climate factors and to enhance the government's ability to support work, road safety and resilience to climate factors.
The World Bank press release, obtained by Fresh News on May 26, 2022, states that the project will help build roads and bridges in flooded areas and increase government capacity in Response to natural disasters caused by the weather.
The project will benefit about 5.5 million people, 52 percent of whom are women in seven provinces, and those who use the road network. Most government officials from the Ministry of Rural Development will receive training through this project on disaster risk management, climate change adaptation and post-disaster needs assessment.
Maryam Salim, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia, said: “With the strong commitment of the Ministry of Rural Development of the Royal Government of Cambodia, this project will help strengthen rural road infrastructure. "And resilient to climate change, while at the same time protecting vulnerable people from natural disasters that no one can foresee."
Cambodia is a country that is very vulnerable to the negative effects of climate change, especially floods and droughts, and rural road infrastructure is also very vulnerable to the risks of natural disasters caused by climate change. .
Rural road network is very important for the daily life of rural communities, and damage to parts of the road network can make life difficult for rural families and hinder access to basic public services such as markets, Schools and hospitals.
The devastation caused by the floods in 2020 underscores Cambodia's need for more investment in disaster risk management and the vulnerability of key road infrastructure.
The project will be implemented by the Ministry of Rural Development, which has been active in increasing the resilience of construction and road maintenance standards. This project will continue from the first phase of the Southeast Asia Disaster Risk Management Project in Cambodia, with a focus on expanding the climate-resilient rural road network and expanding the Ministry of Rural Development's financial planning to support it. Resistance to natural disasters.
Ensuring the resilience of rural road infrastructure is a priority of the Ministry of Rural Development in line with the Cambodian Rural Development Strategy and Action Plan 2019-2023, which defines the improvement of the quality and resilience of rural roads as one of the programs. Its four priority programs.
The new financing package will strengthen the Ministry's capacity for preparedness, response and post-disaster recovery, and to support pre-planning and flood risk management in the affected Tonle Sap region. Severe damage from floods in 2020. The new grant will also further strengthen the management and technical capacity of the Ministry of Rural Development in implementing the project.
