Malaysian chicken export ban raises concerns in Singapore
SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Malaysia's move to ban chicken exports to curb rising prices and fight domestic shortages has raised concerns, with neighboring Singapore warning of supply disruptions. According to the AFP news agency.
The Southeast Asian country was the last country to curb food exports as prices rose after India banned wheat exports and Indonesia temporarily suspended palm oil exports.
"The government pays close attention to the current (low-level) increase in prices and supply of chicken as a serious problem that is affecting the population," the prime minister said in a statement. Malaysia.
The move comes amid growing concerns in Malaysia, but it has raised concerns in Singapore, which relies heavily on Malaysia for food supplies.
About one-third of Singapore's chicken imports will come from Malaysia in 2021, AFP reported, citing a statement issued by the Singapore Food Agency.
The agency warned of "temporary disruptions in the supply of frozen chicken" but added that it was working with importers to reduce the impact. "We also advise consumers to buy only what they need," the agency said.
CNA reports that some chicken stalls at Singapore's fresh produce market have warned that they may be temporarily closed due to the ban.
Malaysia also exports chicken to other markets, including Thailand, Japan and Hong Kong.
