Thailand likely to benefit from neighboring bans
BANGKOK: Thailand, a major exporter of sugar, chicken and rice, is likely to benefit greatly from agricultural exports, which its neighbors have restricted to protect its exports. Local consumers. According to the Bangkok Post.
In recent days, Malaysia has banned chicken exports and India has imposed restrictions on sugar exports. Rising food protection and rising commodity prices are good news for Thai producers who are enjoying a good harvest this year after the drought.
Global food prices have soared for several years as the economy recovers from an epidemic amid Russia's invasion of Ukraine, which has hampered the flow of agricultural goods.
"Thailand is unlikely to suffer from food shortages like other countries because Thailand is a global kitchen," he said.
According to the Bangkok Post, the Sugar Industry Association has forecast that Thai sugar exports will increase by at least 40% in the 2021-2022 season. International commodity prices have risen about 25 percent in the past year, and measures to restrict India's sugar exports could provide further impetus for Thai exports.
Thailand's rice exports, Thailand's largest agricultural export, are projected by the Thai government to reach a four-year high of at least 8 million tonnes this year.
Chicken is the second highest-earning commodity in Thailand's food exports after rice, and Malaysia's ban should be beneficial to Thailand. The Thai Chicken Exporters Association is estimating that exports will reach 950,000 tonnes this year from 930,000 tonnes in 2021.
Reuters also cited an order issued by the Indian government that the government has asked exporters to apply for permits for any export business between June 1 and October 31, Reuters reported. Also.
India is the world's largest producer of sugar and the second largest exporter after Brazil. India is planning to curb sugar exports to maintain domestic prices and ensure a stable supply in the domestic market, Reuters reported in March.
London sugar prices rose more than 1% after India's decision.
"The government is concerned about food inflation and that is why the government is trying to secure a supply of sugar," said a Mumbai-based trader who works with a world-class sugar company. Enough in the country to supply the festive season.
Exporters also said the decision to allow 10 million tonnes of sugar exporters would help India sell a fair amount of sugar to the world market.
India initially planned to reduce sugar exports to 8 million tonnes, but the government later decided to allow an increase to 10 million tonnes as sugar output is estimated to increase. According to Reuters.
