Malaysia bans chicken exports to maintain domestic supply
The Malaysian state news agency Bernama recently reported a shortage of local poultry as traders planned to stop farming operations over the weekend.
The ban is likely to have a significant impact on Singapore, where one-third of the country's supply is imported from Malaysia, as well as Thailand, Brunei, Japan and Hong Kong.
The ban is the latest move by protectionist groups as countries around the world fight rising food prices, partly driven by the war in Ukraine, which has reduced food supplies. Recently, Indonesia has temporarily banned palm oil exports, India has tightened wheat exports, and Serbia and Kazakhstan have set quotas on grain exports.
The Malaysian Competition Commission is investigating reports of traders controlling the price and output of chicken among major companies, promising to take strict action against any company found to be in breach, Bloomberg reports. Subvert supply.
