(Svay Rieng): Many modern motorcycles of 2022 were burned but the skeletons in the factory "Siko" in the Manhattan Special Economic Zone, Bavet City, Svay Rieng Province, due to possible electric shock.
According to a report from workers told Fresh News in Svay Rieng province, a fire engulfed 41 motorcycles in the Siko factory in the Mehathen Special Economic Zone in Bavet City at 1:40 p.m. This afternoon, May 24, 2022.
Sources from the same workers confirmed that before the incident, the factory workers went to work normally, suddenly there were flames and smoke coming from the motorcycle stand, there was a surprise, while the security guards took down the fire hose to shoot. On the motorcycle, but could not control the situation, so many motorcycles burned only the skeleton.
By 3:30 pm on the same day, the fire was completely extinguished and the authorities as well as the factory are looking for additional causes in addition to the electric shock, which led to the fire quickly burned many motorcycles. Heavy rains cause salt production to reach nearly 30,000 tons, facing a shortage of imports!
(Phnom Penh): The weather factor combined with the rainy season has arrived, causing heavy rainfall, causing this year's salt production to receive less yield compared to 2021. This climate problem pushes the supply of salt in short supply and will be able to import salt from abroad to meet demand.
Sok Kim Choeun, director of the Kampot Provincial Department of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, told Fresh News on May 24, 2022 that salt production was completed this May, reaching nearly 30,000 tonnes while salt was in stock. There are only 2-3 thousand tons. With nearly 30,000 tons of salt, it cannot supply enough domestic demand, requiring imports.
"About 30,000 tons of salt can not supply enough domestic demand. Currently, the Ministry of Industry and Handicrafts has allowed companies to import salt to supply domestically," he said.
Please be informed that Kampot and Kep provinces are the location of salt production in Cambodia, with salt production from the end of December to May, while the area for salt production in Kampot province is 3,726.02 hectares. In 2019, Cambodia faced a shortage of salt and imported about 30,000 tons of salt from China for domestic supply.
