Winners of Public Speaking Contest: Raising Tobacco Taxes as Best Measures to Reduce Tobacco Mortality
(Phnom Penh): The first winner in the final public speaking competition under the theme "Measures to increase tobacco tax" Ms. Sim Srey Leap claims that raising tobacco tax is the best measure to help reduce the rate of illness The death toll from tobacco use has been reduced and also contributes to protecting the environment from tobacco production and consumption, which is the theme of World No Tobacco Day 2022.
Candidate Sim Srey Leap stated that although raising tobacco taxes was seen as a win-win measure, there were still some obstacles to prevent a tobacco tax increase in Cambodia. Cambodia as well, which is related to the various misconceptions raised by tobacco companies and another point of concern for those who decide to raise tobacco taxes.
The young man added that, of course, it did not agree with some of the comments made by the tobacco company. On the contrary, raising tobacco taxes is a win-win situation for the Royal Government and the Cambodian people. Increasing the tobacco tax has the greatest benefit: the government will benefit from the state tax revenue to support the country's economy, especially after the huge cost of preventing Kovid-19.
The first place winner added that if you look at the tobacco tax rate in Cambodia, it is only about 25% - 31% of the retail price, which is still very low compared to the rate recommended by the health organization. The world and the World Bank, and also a lower rate than countries in the ASEAN region.
ASEAN countries such as Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, Myanmar, Singapore, the Philippines and Thailand all have tariffs on tobacco products above 50% of retail prices. In fact, the tax rate in Thailand has been increased to 63% at present, while the Philippines has been taxed at 46% -49%, which has helped increase tax revenue for various expenses, especially health care. More people.
Candidate Sim Srey Leap also stated that the use of tobacco products is a burden for the government to solve because the use of tobacco products causes people to get sick and die, of which about 15,000 Cambodians died due to smoking. In addition, tobacco use costs an estimated $ 649 million, or 3 percent of Cambodia's gross domestic product.
