(Phnom Penh): The United Nations World Food Program (WFP) in collaboration with Green Trade and the Cambodian Rice Federation has organized a two-day technical training course on the incorporation of micronutrients (vitamins) in rice to increase nutritional value. In the diet.
According to a press release obtained by Fresh News on May 23, 2022, production managers from 15 mills participated in the training, which demonstrated the practical implementation of the process. Combine installation and how to use equipment and control and quality assurance.
Sok Silo, Secretary-General of the Council for Agricultural and Rural Development, said: "This training is a step forward in supporting the national agenda on the inclusion of micronutrients in food and responding to the recommendations of the dialogue. "On the 2021 food system on the trade in micronutrient-rich foods."
"I believe this is a major step towards solving the problem of malnutrition in a country where most people eat a lot of food every day," he said. "Cambodia is among the countries that consume the most rice in the region."
Mr. Song Saran, President of the Cambodian Rice Federation, considers this training as an important program for Cambodia to join the Royal Government to improve nutrition for the poor in Cambodia.
He said that the inclusion of micronutrients in rice is an important part of the priority and most necessary food for Cambodia, which is also a strategy of the Royal Government on food security system.
Rice is a good source of energy, but it lacks essential micronutrients such as vitamins, iron and calcium found in other foods such as fruits and vegetables. As a result, most Cambodians are affected by micronutrient deficiencies that can affect their health, productivity and learning ability.
A study of the economic consequences of malnutrition in Cambodia found that the cost of malnutrition was between $ 250 million and $ 400 million a year, of which more than $ 134 million was lost due to micronutrient deficiencies.
Mam Borath, director of the Department of Nutrition Promotion at the Ministry of Planning, said: "Adding vitamins or minerals to rice safely improves the nutritional quality of rice and can be an effective way to combat micronutrient deficiencies. "People are poor without having to change their habits and tastes."
He said that according to a 2017 study by the World Food Program, one-fifth of households in Cambodia lack access to adequate nutrition at the lowest possible prices. Since 2010, the World Food Program (WFP) has been the government's leading partner in micronutrient incorporation into rice.
Early studies have shown that micronutrient-rich rice is popular among teachers, parents and students across the country. Later, a study conducted with more than 9,000 students confirmed the positive effects of micronutrient-rich rice on improving the health and thinking ability of children in rural areas.
As a result, the Royal Government approved the use of micronutrient rice in the school feeding program in 2016. Three years later, the World Food Program and Green Trade have mixed micronutrients for the first time in the country, demonstrating a breakthrough as well as paving the way for current work supporting local mills to implement micronutrient blending. This nation.
