Although the four-month pepper harvest went well, traders in the sector expressed concern about the challenges of pepper harvesting due to heavy rains and labor shortages.
Markny, president of the Cambodian Pepper and Spices Federation, said the pepper harvest was currently facing a number of challenges due to rainfall and a shortage of manpower, but so far the harvest had reached 70. The percentage of total pepper is more than 20,000 tons.
"The weather has caused the current price of pepper to drop to only 13,000 riel per kilogram, while the harvest during February was 15,000-16,000 riel per kilogram," he said. "The fall in prices is due to the over-harvesting of farmers, which has led farmers to harvest more pepper and sell it at a cheaper price."
However, he expects pepper prices to recover during September-October, as pepper is less abundant, so sales will be higher.
