Chinese Foreign Ministry Spokeswoman Jiang Yu's Regular Press Conference on Taiwan's Participation in the Accession of Taiwan to the World Health Assembly
(Beijing): The 75th World Health Assembly will be held from May 22 to 28. The Foreign Affairs Bureau of the Taiwan Regional Authority has expressed "regret and dissatisfaction" with the fact that Taiwan has not been invited to the General Assembly.
Many countries have recently sent a letter to the World Health Organization in support of the Chinese government's decision not to allow Taiwan to participate in the annual World Health Assembly and not to have any discussions about it. No issues related to Taiwan at this General Assembly.
Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin said in response on May 21 that the issue of Taiwan's participation in international organizations, including the activities of the World Health Organization, must be addressed in accordance with Chinese principles. One. UN General Assembly Resolution 2758 and World Health Assembly Resolution 25.1 confirm this basic principle.
The Chinese government attaches great importance to the health and well-being of the Taiwanese people and has appropriated Taiwan's participation in global health affairs under the preconditions of the one-China policy.
In order to safeguard national sovereignty and territorial integrity, as well as to uphold the rigor and prestige of the relevant resolutions of the UN General Assembly and the World Health Assembly, China does not approve Taiwan's participation in this year's World Health Assembly. No.
China's decision has been widely supported and understood by the international community. It reaffirmed that the one-China policy is the aspiration of the international community and a common trend, and fully demonstrates the correct position of the overwhelming majority of countries that upholds justice on issues related to Taiwan at the World Health Assembly. He too.
