During a meeting with the Cambodian community living in Europe in Zurich, Switzerland, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that if the prince was ready to cut off land to Vietnam as alleged, it would not be an effort to spend even a penny on negotiations. To date no.
Regarding the demarcation of the border with Vietnam, Samdech Techo Hun Sen reaffirmed that the demarcation is now 90 percent complete, while the two sides are still negotiating to reach the 100 percent goal in the near future.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen reiterated that Cambodia and Vietnam have agreed to request French experts to help transfer the map from 1 / 1,000 scale carbon map to 1 / 50,000 scale UTM map for clarity and ease of border demarcation. .
At the same time, Samdech Techo Prime Minister also stated that after the peace ended the war in 1998, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set two main goals: first: to turn the former battlefield into a commercial and development zone and secondly: Become a border with all neighboring countries with a border of peace, friendship, cooperation and development.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen claims that now the first goal has been achieved up to 95 percent, which turns the former battlefield into a development area, while the remaining 5 percent is the area with mines and unexploded ordnance. The government is working hard on this.
Separately, along Cambodia's border with neighboring Vietnam, Thailand and Laos, the prince claimed that Cambodia demarcated the border with Laos about 86 percent, with Vietnam 84 percent (6 percent agreed 90 percent) and with Thailand recognized the old pillars found See and set up a joint commission to continue this work.
Although not 100% complete, Samdech Techo Hun Sen stressed that this issue should not be left as an enemy of trade and relations between the country and the people and the people.
