Here are the key details regarding this important international forum:
The World Economic Forum (WEF), a non-profit fund based in Geneva, Switzerland, is recognized by Swiss authorities as an international entity. The mission of the World Economic Forum is to promote trade, politics and set the agenda of regional and global industry-focused meetings.
The organization also holds about six to eight regional meetings a year in regions such as Latin America and East Asia, as well as additional annual meetings in China and the United Arab Emirates. In addition, the World Economic Forum conducts research reports and links its members in a clear direction.
* History of the World Economic Forum:
The forum was created in 1971 under the initiative of Klaus Schwab, a professor of business at the University of Geneva, born in Germany. The forum was originally named the "European Management Forum". In 1987, the forum was renamed the World Economic Forum and aims to broaden its horizons towards international conflict resolution.
* Management:
The forum is headquartered in Cologny, Switzerland, New York, Beijing and Tokyo. On October 10, 2016, the forum announced the opening of the Fourth Industrial Revolution Center in San Francisco. According to sources from the forum, the center serves as a platform for the exchange of science and technology to change people's lives, work and communication from one place to another.
The World Economic Forum is trying to become a neutral institution, not involved in partisan politics or for the benefit of any one nation. The forum is committed to moving the world forward. During the annual meeting, about 2,500 participants from about 100 countries gathered in Davos.
Of those, about 1,500 are business leaders, and the other 1,000 are owners of the world's top companies. Other participants included 219 public figures, 40 state or government leaders, 64 officials, 30 senior officials representing 30 international organizations and 10 ambassadors. In addition, 149 analysts and leaders of educational institutions, 15 religious leaders and 11 federal leaders attended.
The Technology Reform Program regularly prepares programs to recognize companies that innovate and develop new technologies. It will reward about 30 to 50 companies each year. Since 2000, the program has presented awards to more than 400 companies from five continents.
During the 1990s, it, along with other organizations, including the G7, the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund, came under heavy criticism from anti-globalization activists who considered globalization to be a source of poverty and destruction. Environment.
