Climate change causes cashew exports from Cambodia to Vietnam to drop by more than 40% (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) Cashew nut exports to Vietnam fall by more than 40% in the first four months of 2022
He explained that this year, the Cambodian Cashew Association estimates that cashew production has dropped by up to 50% due to weather conditions, meaning that the association's member farmers can only produce between 570,000 and 600,000 tons, of which more than 500,000. Tons were sold to Vietnamese traders and more than 10 tons remained in stock.
He said that the past assessment of the association is rarely wrong in both quantity and price because the association receives information from the world market and weather information, as well as directly to the plantations in the 10 target provinces.
This year, he said, farmers have suffered heavy losses as the global crisis has pushed up the prices of fertilizers, fertilizers and pesticides as cashew prices have fallen. Currently, the price of wet cashew nuts has dropped to between 2,000 riel and 3,000 riel, and the price of dried cashew nuts is between 5,800 riel and 6,800 riel, depending on the quality of the seeds.
He said that this problem has led some farmers with small cashew trees, two to three years old, to decide to plow and other farmers to cut down the trees to the cassava land instead, which he estimates is about 100,000 hectares. . As of the beginning of May 2022, he estimates that there are only about 700,000 hectares of cashew plantations left.
According to data obtained by the Cambodian Cashew Association via satellite, the country has about 800,000 hectares of cashew plantations, but the official data is only about 490,000 hectares. Representative of Mr. Kun Nhim Meets and Recommends to CPP Representatives and Youth in Kien Svay District
