TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan's economy shrank for the first time in two quarters between January and March as Kovid-19 prevention hit the services sector and rising commodity prices put new pressure. Which raises concerns about further declines. According to Reuters.
The recession poses a challenge for Japanese Prime Minister Fumio Kishida's plan to achieve economic growth and the distribution of economic benefits among the population under his "new capitalist" agenda. This has led to fears of an economic stalemate and rising inflation.
The economy of the world's third-largest economy shrank 1 percent year-on-year in the quarter, citing figures released by the Cabinet Office. January-March was down 1.8 percent from the previous quarter, less than economists had forecast. At quarterly rates, the Japanese economy contracted 0.2 percent, compared with market estimates of 0.4 percent.
The recession could put pressure on Kishida to step up stimulus measures after spending an additional 2.7 trillion yen ($ 20.86 billion).
"The blockade in China and the US rate hike, as well as the Ukraine crisis, could put a strain on external demand," he said. Declining household and business incomes, which could worsen the situation, could hamper the recovery in domestic demand. ” Dismantle the network cable, change the equipment, easy to the road widening site
