Ministry of Justice Spokesman Reveals Legal Concerns About Throwing Shoes or Harassing Foreign Leaders in the United States, France and Cambodia (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): In the United States, France and Cambodia all have laws that punish For committing the crime of throwing shoes or hurting foreign leaders as an internationally protected individual.
Jean Malin, secretary of state and spokesman for the Ministry of Justice, explained that the case of throwing shoes and hurting foreign leaders, as internationally protected individuals in these countries, could be charged with violating the foreign head of state or Committing violence, etc.
On this issue, Mr. Jean Malin explained as follows:
"Today I would like to share legal information related to the case of the US President who was kicked by an Iraqi journalist and the case of the French President who was slapped by a person who was published in some foreign newspaper articles for the public to know: Under:
Providing special protection to foreign officials is not a new concept or a new thing, and not only in the United States and Iraq, but also in France and Switzerland.
This is according to the advertisement of Slate magazine on December 23, 2008 and some related news articles.
* Cambodian criminal law
First, Article 30, point 2 of the Anti-Terrorism Law:
Intentional violence against an internationally protected person is punishable by seven to 15 years in prison. In cases where the violence is aggravated by the violence resulting in the death of the victim, the perpetrator will be sentenced to 7 to 15 years in prison.
"Second, Article 33 of the Anti-Terrorism Law stipulates that a person who threatens to commit an offense against an internationally protected person shall be punished by imprisonment for five to 10 years." Mr. Hun Many: "Age is not a determinant of success in work, respect, cooperation, value each other to achieve results is a determinant of true success."
