Indonesia, the world's largest exporter of palm oil, has stopped exporting crude palm oil and other palm oil-related products since April 28 in a bid to curb rising domestic cooking oil prices. Affect the global vegetable oil market.
Reuters reports that Indonesian farmers marched to protest, also accompanied by palm oil trucks, to the office of the Indonesian Ministry of Economic Coordination, a policy-making body of the Indonesian government.
The news agency reported that protesters held banners reading "Malaysian farmers smile, Indonesian farmers suffer".
Malaysia is the second largest producer of palm oil and recently said it intends to supply a market that is short of supplies due to Indonesia's export ban.
The small group of farmers said the protesters also planned to march to the presidential palace. Similar protests were held in 22 other provinces.
Data from the Indonesian Ministry of Commerce showed that as of last Friday, the average price of cooking oil was 17,300 rupees per liter. According to Reuters.
