Russia expels two Finnish embassy staff in retaliation
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Two staff members of the Finnish embassy in Russia will leave the country in response to similar measures taken by Helsinki, Moscow said on Tuesday. This is according to the release by AFP on Tuesday, May 17, 2022.
Russia has summoned the Finnish ambassador to Moscow and expressed strong opposition to the expulsion of two Russian diplomats in April, the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a statement.
Russia has also accused Finland of choosing a "confrontational path" with it, supplying weapons to Ukraine and covering up Ukrainian nationalist crimes against civilians.
"The Russian side has decided on the unacceptability of further accommodation in the Russian Federation of two staff members of the Finnish Embassy in Moscow," the Foreign Ministry said.
Since President Vladimir Putin sent troops to Ukraine on February 24, the West has expelled dozens of Russian diplomats and Russia has retaliated in similar actions.
Finland, which has a 1,340-kilometer land border with Russia, has recently reaffirmed its intention to join NATO after decades of non-alignment with the military.
(Phnom Penh): On May 17, 2022, the Cambodian Red Cross continued its mission to target Oddar Meanchey and Ratanakkiri provinces, which decided to provide humanitarian aid to a total of 678 most vulnerable families.
