This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen met with a group of Samdech Techo Youth Volunteer Doctors
(Phnom Penh): After completing a busy mission on American waters, this morning, Tuesday, May 17, 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, continued to meet with a group of Samdech Techo Youth Volunteer Doctors.
The Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association (SCYY) was established on December 17, 2012 under the direct initiative of Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen. Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association is led by Dr. Hun Manet and Dr. Pech Chanmony in order to provide medical treatment to the poor throughout the country without any political affiliation.
Since its establishment, the Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association, which is made up of a team of doctors from all specialties and public and private institutions, has been involved in screening and treating people, especially in rural areas.
According to the report, since its inception, doctors from the Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association have treated more than 330,000 poor people across the country. More than 6,000 critically ill patients were sent to Phnom Penh for treatment and with various partners. Among the critically ill were 56 patients with severe heart disease.
They have worked tirelessly and wholeheartedly in the fight against Kovid 19, regardless of time. During the mission, some doctors have to work almost 24 hours and some doctors are unconscious during the mission.
According to the report, Samdech Techo Youth Volunteer Doctors treated 25,842 Kovid 19 patients, sampled more than 710,000 Kovid 19 patients and vaccinated 19 Kovid 19 people in the capital. Phnom Penh has more than 300,000 people.
The activities of Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors have become an important part of protecting the lives of the people from the outbreak of Kovid 19 and also a big part in helping the Royal Government to overcome Kovid 19.
India bans wheat exports as heatwaves hit crops, push up prices
Mumbai: India bans wheat exports as heat wave cuts output and domestic prices hit record highs According to Reuters.
The Indian government will still allow exports to companies with letters of credit already issued and for exports to countries requesting supplies "to meet their food security needs".
Reuters quoted senior Indian government officials as saying at a news conference that India's ban on exports was not permanent and could be changed. Repeat.
Buyers around the world are relying on supplies from the world's second-largest wheat producer after exports from the Black Sea fell after Russia's Feb. 24 invasion. On Ukraine. Prior to the ban, India intended to export 10 million tonnes this year.
The officials added that there had been no significant drop in wheat yields this year, but uncontrolled exports had led to a rise in domestic prices.
