Thai government to reduce tariffs on diesel by 5 baht per liter
BANGKOK: The Thai Ministry of Finance is considering a request from government officials to reduce the tariff on diesel by 5 baht per liter as the current tax cut expires on May 20. May. According to the Bangkok Post.
The Bangkok Post quoted an unnamed Thai Ministry of Finance source as saying that the tax cut was aimed at curbing rising inflation.
The Thai Ministry of Finance believes that the war between Russia and Ukraine will continue, pushing up world oil prices. The source said he saw the loss of revenue from the tariff reduction as worth the sacrifice if it could ease the cost of living for the public as rising inflation is now a major issue. Most.
The Thai government has decided to reduce the tax on diesel by 3 baht per liter from a total of 5.99 baht per liter from February 18 to May 20 to ease inflationary pressures. Public and private sectors.
The tariff cuts cost the customs department a total of about 17.1 billion baht (about $ 488 million). Any reduction in tariffs on 1 baht per liter of diesel is estimated to cost the Thai government about 1.9 billion baht a month.
