Asia's major economies warn of war risks, normalize monetary policy
TOKYO (Reuters) - Leaders of East Asia's major economies on Thursday warned of risks to the region's economic outlook and pledged to remain committed to market stability and strong fiscal policy. According to Reuters.
The joint statement came after the online annual meeting of the cabinet and governors.
Officials from Japan, China and South Korea have reaffirmed their commitment to supporting financial market stability and long-term fiscal sustainability.
"We must remain vigilant against the escalating risks facing the regional economic recovery ... in addition to the ongoing Russia-Ukraine conflict and the escalation of hostilities," the statement said. "Normalize (normalize) monetary policy ahead of expectations in some developed countries."
"These factors could pose a downside risk to the regional economic outlook, causing changes in financial markets and capital flows," the statement said.
The joint statement comes amid concerns that a rise in US interest rates and a decline related to central bank assets have pushed the dollar higher. This has raised expectations for capital outflows from some emerging economies and rising dollar debt burden in developing countries. According to Reuters.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Instructs Companies to Help US Confuse Cambodia's Foreign Policy
(Washington, USA): Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen has instructed US consulting and persuasion companies to explain to US leadership that there should be no confusion over Cambodia's foreign policy, which could lead to tensions between Cambodia and the United States. .
Samdech Techo Hun Sen's request was made when he allowed Mr. Scott David Parven, a representative of the American consulting and lobbying company, and his team to pay a courtesy call and discuss some work. This American consulting and persuasion company is a company that Cambodia has used to serve to facilitate and improve the relationship between Cambodia and the United States.
Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister, said that during the meeting, Mr. Scott David Pavin informed the Prime Minister that his team had worked with the Cambodian Embassy in the United States to promote relations between the two countries. Cambodia and the United States are doing well.
In addition, his company has been in contact with US leaders in Congress, as well as the government, to help the United States understand Cambodia's position on bilateral relations, ASEAN, and international affairs. In particular, Cambodia's position relates to Russia's invasion of Ukraine, climate change, trade, and Cambodia's humanitarian efforts.
According to Scott, through meetings with leaders of Congress and the US government, he highlighted that what Cambodia has done in the past is a good thing that should be celebrated and valued for Cambodia.
In front of the management and colleagues of the American lobby company, Samdech Techo Hun Sen highlighted that the relationship between Cambodia and the United States today has reached an unprecedented level in the history of relations between the two countries.
Samdech Techo also said that the United States has helped many developments in Cambodia, such as promoting trade, investment, demining, training and the provision of vaccines to fight Kovid 19. Samdech Techo said that the United States should join in rejoicing in the achievements that the United States has helped Cambodia.
