National Bank of Cambodia: Inflation is likely to rise by 4% this year due to rising food and fuel prices due to the effects of the Russia-Ukraine war
(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia said that in 2022, inflation is expected to increase by 4% due to the increase in food and fuel prices due to the effects of the Russia-Ukraine war and transportation costs. There is a disruption of the supply chain and an increase in domestic demand.
Mr. Sou Sannisith said that the situation of Kovid-19 has been steadily improving as the Royal Government has taken timely measures to control it, especially the vaccination of Kovid-19 to more than 90 percent of the population, and has introduced Supportive policies to support the livelihoods of vulnerable people and support economic activities.
"These factors will enable Cambodia to achieve a high economic growth rate. In 2022, Cambodia is expected to achieve a strong economic growth rate of 5.3 percent, supported by growth in the manufacturing sector 9.6 percent," he said. "The construction sector, 1.2 percent, agriculture 1.2 percent and tourism are expected to grow 6 percent positively after being hit hard two years ago."
"Inflation is expected to rise by 4 percent due to rising food, fuel prices, the effects of the Russia-Ukraine war and transportation costs, as well as supply chain disruptions and rising domestic demand," he added. "
