Ministry of Economy opens training course on state property management procedures for professional civil servants of ministries and institutions
The opening ceremony was presided over by Dr. Hean Sahip, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Ministry of Economy and Finance Capacity Building Working Group, accompanied by HE Lok Chumteav, Undersecretary of State and leaders of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, as well as trainers. Lecturers and trainees.
Speaking on the occasion, he emphasized that the management of state assets is an important task in reforming the management of public finances, with state assets as a source of non-tax revenue that contributes to increasing the national budget revenue.
At the same time, the management of state assets is the effective management, use and management of state assets, accountability, transparency and sustainability in order to contribute to the economic, social, environmental and cultural development.
In addition, state property is an essential resource of production factors as a basis for diversifying production and services to promote business activities, investment, job creation and sustainable economic growth, contributing to socio-economic development and prosperity.
He emphasized that training on state property management procedures is an important part of building and developing the capacity of civil servants on relevant skills in public financial management to meet the needs of Cambodia to support and implement. The reform of the Royal Government's public financial management, in particular, aims to support the effective and productive management of state assets.
He added that within the framework of the implementation of the Public Financial Management Reform Program, the Ministry of Economy and Finance, as the State Asset Management Authority, has established and implemented the Information Technology Management System (SARMIS) phase. The first was in December 2020.
The SARMIS system will allow users to update the value of state assets more easily, the transmission of data from state property authorities to state property management authorities quickly, especially the management of state property inventory to be more modern, accurate, transparent. Efficiency and accountability in line with international best practices.
