US reaffirms commitment to defend Japan, South Korea in tensions with North Korea
TOKYO (Reuters) - The U.S. military headquarters in the Indo-Pacific region said it was in close consultation with allies and partners after North Korea tested a ballistic missile on Saturday. This was released by NHK on Sunday, May 8, 2022.
In its statement, the US Indo-Pacific Military Command assessed that the ballistic missile test did not pose an immediate threat to US troops or territory or US allies. But called the test an act of instability caused by North Korea's illegal weapons program and said Washington's commitment to defending South Korea and Japan had not changed.
On Wednesday, North Korea also test-fired a ballistic missile into the sea east of its country after Pyongyang vowed to develop its nuclear forces as quickly as possible. In total, North Korea has test-fired 15 missiles this year, up from four in 2020 and eight in 2021. Mr. Kem Sokha led the delegation to pay tribute to the late Samdech Udom Tep Nhean Hun Neng
