The world's second-largest liquefied natural gas (LNG) importer is expected to face tight electricity markets again in the summer and winter, according to Reuters data and trade sources. Turning to fuel for power plants due to the crisis in Ukraine has pushed up gas and coal prices.
Europe is stockpiling fuel amid rising tensions with its largest gas supplier, Russia. Moscow is asking for payment for its gas purchases in Russian rupees, otherwise it will cut off supplies.
A Mitsubishi spokesman said the company traded crude oil to meet customer needs, but did not comment on the deal.
Traders who closely monitor Japan's oil market say crude oil is usually burned at Japanese power plants.
BEIJING: The central banks of China and Russia will discuss the use and upgrading of their respective national payment systems in Beijing, according to Beijing's envoy to Moscow. According to Reuters.
Mir and UnionPay payment systems are among the few options left for Russians to pay abroad since the Bank of Russia was isolated from the global financial system in response to what Moscow calls "special military operations." Its in Ukraine.
China has refused to condemn Russia's actions in Ukraine and has criticized unprecedented Western sanctions on Moscow. The two countries have strengthened ties in recent years, including announcing an "unlimited partnership" in February.
The Chinese envoy said that China will use a clearing bank for yuan transactions on Russian territory to ensure the sustainable development of national trade, which Russia expects to reach 200 billion US dollars by 2024.
He also said that China and Russia have not been advocating for the rejection of the use of the US dollar and the euro in bilateral trade settlements. According to Reuters.
Britain announces $ 1.60 billion in military aid to Ukraine
British Prime Minister Boris Johnson has been among a number of Western leaders who have expressed full support for Ukraine, which has been battling Russian troops since the outbreak of war on February 24 in the city. London has sent anti-tank missiles, air defense systems and other weapons to Ukraine.
