KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia, the world's second-largest producer of palm oil, has said it plans to take advantage of global vegetable oil shortages and political tensions in Europe to regain market share. Buyers have avoided buying Malaysian palm oil due to environmental concerns. According to Reuters.
Coconut oil has been used to make everything from lip balm to noodles, but the world's two largest producers of palm oil, Indonesia and Malaysia, have faced boycotts after being accused of clearing rubbish. Rainforests and exploit migrant workers for the rapid expansion of their plantations.
But retailers such as Britain's Icelandic supermarket, which removed palm oil from its branded food in 2018, have been forced to re-use the same oil in recent months due to widespread vegetable oil shortages. Globalization caused by the Russia-Ukraine war and Indonesia's palm oil export ban.
"It's time for us to step up our efforts to combat the negative propaganda that undermines the credibility of palm oil, and for us to highlight the many health benefits that coconut oil has," she said. .
Zuraida added that global vegetable oil prices are likely to remain high in the first half of 2022, and that EU demand is expected to increase in the near future due to a shortage of sunflower oil supplies. Soybeans.
Uncertainty over the supply of sunflower oil due to Russia's invasion of Ukraine has boosted demand for palm oil and soybean oil as importers seek alternative oil options, boosting vegetable oil prices.
Zuraida added that Malaysia will benefit from this change in demand and will work hard and campaign hard to fill the long-term global supply shortage. According to Reuters. CIA Director: Putin believes Russia must win Ukraine war
