Japan says it will find it difficult to implement restrictions on oil imports from Russia (Video inside) (Tokyo): Japan's economy, trade and industry minister says Japan will face "difficulties" in implementing immediate Measures to cut oil imports from Russia over invasion of Ukraine According to Reuters.
Minister Koichi Hagiuda made the remarks on Thursday during a visit to Washington after the European Commission on Wednesday proposed tougher sanctions against Moscow, including sanctions on crude oil. .
US President Joe Biden has said he will discuss further sanctions against Russia with other leaders from the Group of Seven (G7) nations this week that could put pressure on Japan. Beware of the effects of restrictions on the oil trade.
In a meeting with US Secretary of Commerce Gina Raimondo, the two agreed on the principles of semi-replication equipment cooperation aimed at diversifying chip production capacity, promoting transparency, immediate response to shortages and research and development.
"At a time when the world is destabilizing after the war in Ukraine, coordination with like-minded countries is even more important," he said. "The talks (today) served as an important step towards boosting Japan-US relations."
For the last fiscal year ending March, Japanese oil imports from Russia accounted for 4 percent of total oil imports, 9 percent of natural gas imports and 11 percent of coal imports. According to Reuters.
