The meeting was held to discuss the search and repatriation plans for the remains of Vietnamese volunteers and experts in 2022 and beyond. General Knowledge: How did the Guam Islands in the western Pacific become US-owned?
The only reason the United States came to occupy the Guam Islands, nearly 10,000 kilometers from its home country, was because the United States was at war with Spain at the time. When the US-Spanish War broke out in April 1898, the Guam Islands had been under Spanish rule since the 1600s.
Finally, when both sides became clear that they were enemies, the United States asked the Spanish governor of Guam to surrender, giving him 30 minutes. The Spanish governor agreed, and the game became American property. The U.S. military has been there for just over a day and has left no one in charge of Guam and has even returned the flag.
It was the first and last event between the two sides during the US-Spanish War that took place on the island of Guam and there was no bloodshed. After winning the war, the United States turned Guam into its official territory, and the people of this island, which the US government calls Gammanion, are now American citizens by birth.
