WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The Pentagon on Thursday denied reports that Washington had shared intelligence with Ukraine to target Russian generals. This is according to a quote from The Hill newspaper on Friday, May 6, 2022.
Speaking at a news conference, Pentagon spokesman John Kirby acknowledged that "the United States is providing intelligence from the battlefield to help Ukraine defend its country. But Kiev is self-assembled and makes its own decisions and actions.
Kirby's comments came after the New York Times reported that Washington had provided Ukraine with details of its military movements and locations, as well as information from Russia's mobile headquarters, and that Ukraine had used the information. These combined with personal information to launch artillery attacks and other attacks that killed at least 12 Russian generals.
