(Phnom Penh): Duke Kith Meng, President of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, met with Ms. Marisa Haruta, Representative of the Japan External Trade Organization (JETRO) in Cambodia, to sign a Memorandum of Understanding within the framework of the Council. ASEAN Business Advisory and East Asia Business Council, as well as business promotion cooperation.
This Memorandum of Understanding was made on May 5, 2022 at the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce.
This Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) is aimed at promoting the private sector in trade and supporting regional cooperation within the framework of the ASEAN Business Advisory Council and the East Asia Business Council, headed by Duke Kith Meng Chea. Chairman.
1: Cooperation under the framework of the ASEAN Business Advisory Council and the East Asia Business Council
* Jointly promote trade and investment between Japan and ASEAN / East Asian countries * Propose a report of private companies' recommendations to ASEAN / East Asian leaders * Co-organize the ASEAN Business and Investment Summit and encourage Companies sponsor this summit
2: Cooperation to promote trade * Participate in creating a good business environment to encourage more Japanese private companies to invest in Cambodia * Collaborate to organize business and investment promotion events, including business meetings, seminars, conferences, trade fairs and missions Business and so on.
It should be noted that for more than 10 years, the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and the Japan External Trade Organization have collaborated on many programs together. Both institutions have achieved remarkably fruitful implementation of programs and projects.
Therefore, the signing ceremony of the Memorandum of Understanding is a positive sign that helps to strengthen and deepen the cooperation between the two institutions.
The event was held at the Phnom Penh Hotel and was attended by the leaders of the Ministry of Health, representatives of UNFPA, WHO, representatives of health development partners, relevant institutions, midwives and a total of 150 midwifery students, as well as online Zoom by members of Midwifery branches in 25 capitals and provinces as well.
The celebration of Midwifery Day, May 5, 2022 aims to: * Celebrate and recognize the role - midwifery work on the 28th anniversary of the establishment of the Cambodian Midwifery Association * Provide knowledge about the role of midwives to strengthen primary health care * Promote Sharing knowledge and networking, reproductive health, maternal and child health * Continue to seek support for midwifery investment.
In his message, Dr. Bun Rany Hun Sen also expressed his appreciation and gratitude to all Cambodian midwives who sacrificed everything, including personal and family health, to fulfill the duty to overcome difficulties to participate in preventing the transmission of Kovid virus - 19 and continue to maintain health care for women in general, pregnant women, infants and children to function well on a regular basis.
At the same time, Samdech Kittipritthbandit calls on this brave fighting spirit to stay with Cambodian midwives forever and for all midwives to continue to work:
1: Provide educational counseling to men and women who are about to get married about reproductive health, prevention of infectious diseases, especially prevention of infectious diseases from mother to child 2: Provide care during pregnancy, prevent and detect the consequences in a timely manner 3: Provide Maternity and postnatal care services 4: Provide contraception and other essential services related to midwifery.
At the same time, Samdech Kittipritthbandit would like to call on all pregnant women to go for regular health check-ups by doctors, to go to give birth at a health center or referral hospital, to check the health after delivery and to take the baby for injection. 11 vaccines on a regular basis and take your child to the nearest hospital as soon as possible when the baby or toddler has a fever or abnormalities or difficulty breathing.
"The Ministry of Health will continue to support key interventions in midwifery, in addition to investing in increasing the number of skilled midwives in line with human resource needs in the health sector, including the allocation of work in public health facilities," he said. "And invest in additional skills training and post-basic training."
