Sri Lanka tea exports hit record low in 23 years
COLOMBO (Reuters) - Exports of Sri Lanka's main crisis-affected tea leaves fell to a 23-year low due to a ban on fertilizer imports and the war in Ukraine, official figures showed on Wednesday. According to the AFP news agency.
Tea leaves are the island nation's largest export, earning about $ 1.3 billion a year before the current economic crisis, the worst since independence. 1948.
But last year's ban on fertilizer imports, made in a devastating effort to save foreign currency and avoid debt default, has hit growers hard, with production falling 18 percent for the period. From December 2021 to February 2022 compared to the same period last year.
Exports in the first quarter of 2022 fell to 63.7 million kilograms, down from 69.8 million kilograms in January, AFP reported, citing Sri Lankan customs data. March last year.
This is the lowest level since the first quarter of 1999, when the country exported only 60.3 million kilograms.
Export earnings for the first quarter also fell to $ 287 million, down from $ 338 million.
The ban was lifted in October after a backlash from the industry, but farmers are still unable to access imported fertilizers as the country runs out of dollars at the same time.
