The percentage of oxygen produced in the oceans is difficult to quantify because its production volumes are constantly changing. Scientists can use satellite imagery to track photosynthesis and estimate the amount of photosynthesis that occurs in the oceans, but the use of satellite imagery does not completely tell the situation.
The amount of these tongs also varies with the seasons and due to the amount of nutrients as well as the temperature in the water and many other factors. In addition, studies have shown that the amount of oxygen in some places varies with time and tides.
Remember that although the oceans produce at least 50 percent of the Earth's oxygen supply, the oceans need to use about the same amount of oxygen. Like terrestrial animals and plants, marine animals and plants need oxygen. On the other hand, when they die and rot in the ocean, they also need oxygen.
