Denmark to discard more than 1 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine, which is no longer needed and is about to expire
More than 1.1 million doses of the 19-dose Kovid vaccine will be discarded in the coming weeks as they approach expiration, Copenhagen health officials said Monday. Donation of these vaccines to developing countries has failed. This is according to the publication of Al-Jazeera on Tuesday, May 3, 2022.
According to the Danish Institute of Infectious Diseases (SSI), the epidemic in the northern European country is now under control, and 19 cases of the Kovid vaccine are among the highest in Denmark. About 81 percent of the 5.8 million people received two doses of Kovid 19 vaccine, while nearly 62 percent received a booster dose.
The SSI added: "We now face a global challenge to donate 19 Kovid vaccines due to oversupply of vaccines, as Denmark has already donated 9 million doses and is continuing to work with other EU countries. "To find a country that will accept the rest of the vaccine."
