Taiwan shortens transit time for all passengers despite 19 Kovid cases
(Taipei): Taiwan on Tuesday announced that it will reduce the length of travel for all arrivals from 10 days to 7 days. This was released by the AP on Tuesday, May 3, 2022.
It is the latest easing of Taiwan's latest COVID-19 restrictions aimed at trying to learn to live with the disease and return to normal life, although the number of cases in the community continues to rise.
Taiwan's Disease Control and Prevention Agency says the reduction in the duration of the catalyst, which will take effect next Monday, is due to the short-term onset of symptoms of the new mutation virus Omicron. Along with other factors such as maintaining community communicable disease prevention capacity, socio-economic activities and effective risk management.
However, all arriving passengers will still be required to undergo PCR testing upon arrival in Taiwan, and they will be allowed to leave the catastrophe on the 7th as long as they have a negative test. . On the other hand, a PCR test with a negative result before departure is also needed.
The Taiwanese government has not set a timeline for the full reopening of the border, and restrictions remain in place for those who can travel to the island, except for Taiwanese nationals and foreigners residing there. This can be accessed freely.
